1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,091
Before your next launch, run the one meeting

2
00:00:05,091 --> 00:00:07,000
most teams skip.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:08,692
It's called a premortem.

4
00:00:08,692 --> 00:00:12,077
Don't ask what could go wrong — assume

5
00:00:12,077 --> 00:00:13,346
it already did.

6
00:00:13,346 --> 00:00:16,308
It's six months later, the launch flopped.

7
00:00:16,308 --> 00:00:18,000
Now everyone writes why.

8
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,500
That small flip changes everything.

9
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:24,500
People say the concern they were sitting on.

10
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:26,500
The dependency nobody owned.

11
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:30,000
The assumption everyone agreed with too fast.

12
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,810
Thirty minutes of that, and you walk out

13
00:00:33,810 --> 00:00:37,619
with the two or three risks worth fixing

14
00:00:37,619 --> 00:00:40,000
— while they're still cheap.

15
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,500
What's the one thing that, if your launch

16
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:47,000
failed, you already suspect would be the reason?
